1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:01,650
Coleman: Hi Coleman, how are you?

2
00:00:01,650 --> 00:00:03,100
Coleman: Good, thanks.

3
00:00:03,100 --> 00:00:05,603
Woman: I wanted to have a chat

4
00:00:05,603 --> 00:00:12,397
with you because our friend Josh told me that you've joined a guitar group and it sounds interesting.

5
00:00:12,397 --> 00:00:14,900
I'd really like to learn myself.

6
00:00:14,900 --> 00:00:17,609
Coleman: Why don't you come along?

7
00:00:17,609 --> 00:00:23,800
I'm sure there's room for another person. Woman: Really? So - who runs the classes?

8
00:00:23,800 --> 00:00:29,670
Coleman: He's called a 'coordinator' - his name's Gary Mathieson.

9
00:00:29,670 --> 00:00:36,100
Woman: Let me note that down. Gary.... How do you spell his surname?

10
00:00:36,100 --> 00:00:46,620
Coleman: It's M-A-T-H-I-E-S-O-N.

11
00:00:46,620 --> 00:00:48,670
Woman: Right, thanks.

12
00:00:48,670 --> 00:00:53,240
Coleman: He's retired, actually, but he's a really nice guy

13
00:00:53,240 --> 00:00:56,750
and he used to play in a lot of bands.

14
00:00:56,750 --> 00:01:01,470
Woman: Thanks. So how long have you been going?

15
00:01:01,470 --> 00:01:03,670
Coleman: About a month now.

16
00:01:03,670 --> 00:01:07,770
Woman: And could you play anything before you started?

17
00:01:07,770 --> 00:01:11,050
Coleman: I knew a few chords, but that's all.

18
00:01:11,050 --> 00:01:13,950
Woman: I'm sure everyone will be better than me.

19
00:01:13,950 --> 00:01:16,478
Coleman: That's what I thought, too.

20
00:01:16,478 --> 00:01:22,618
When I first spoke to Gary on the phone, he said it was a class for beginners,

21
00:01:22,618 --> 00:01:25,460
but I was still worried that everyone would be better than me

22
00:01:25,960 --> 00:01:29,120
but we were all equally hopeless.

23
00:01:29,120 --> 00:01:34,220
Woman: Oh, that's reassuring. So where do you meet?

24
00:01:34,220 --> 00:01:39,440
Coleman: Well, when I joined the group, they were meeting in Gary's home,

25
00:01:39,440 --> 00:01:40,860
but as the group got bigger

26
00:01:41,180 --> 00:01:47,270
he decided to book a room at the college in town. I prefer going there.

27
00:01:47,270 --> 00:01:52,760
Woman: I know that place. I used to go to tap dancing classes there

28
00:01:52,760 --> 00:01:55,120
when I was at secondary school.

29
00:01:55,120 --> 00:01:57,060
Woman: I haven't been since, though

30
00:01:57,100 --> 00:02:03,700
and I can't remember what road it's in... is it Lock Street?

31
00:02:03,700 --> 00:02:10,400
Coleman: It's just beyond there at the bottom of New Street near the city roundabout.

32
00:02:10,400 --> 00:02:12,600
Woman: Yes, of course.

33
00:02:12,600 --> 00:02:19,270
Coleman: The guitar club is on the first floor in Room T347.

34
00:02:19,270 --> 00:02:25,620
Woman: Right. And when do you meet? Is it at the weekend?

35
00:02:25,620 --> 00:02:32,300
Coleman: We meet on Thursdays. It used to be 10.30 and that suited me well

36
00:02:32,660 --> 00:02:36,500
but now we meet at 11.

37
00:02:36,500 --> 00:02:39,180
Coleman: The class that's in there before us asked

38
00:02:39,180 --> 00:02:42,500
if they could have the room for another 30 minutes.

39
00:02:42,500 --> 00:02:50,050
Woman: Oh, I see. Well, I'd love to come, but I don't have a guitar.

40
00:02:50,050 --> 00:02:54,537
Coleman: Well, you can always buy a second-hand one.

41
00:02:54,537 --> 00:03:03,511
There's a website called 'The perfect instrument' that sells all kinds of guitars, violins and so on.

42
00:03:03,511 --> 00:03:07,500
I'm sure you'll find something there.

43
00:03:07,500 --> 00:03:10,550
Woman: So what's a typical lesson like with Gary?

44
00:03:10,550 --> 00:03:15,400
Coleman: Well, he always starts by getting us to tune our guitars

45
00:03:16,100 --> 00:03:18,370
That takes about five minutes.

46
00:03:18,370 --> 00:03:19,650
Woman: Uhuh.

47
00:03:19,650 --> 00:03:25,020
Coleman: Some people have an app they use, but others do it by ear

48
00:03:25,560 --> 00:03:28,320
Gary goes round and helps them.

49
00:03:28,320 --> 00:03:30,480
Coleman: And while he's doing that

50
00:03:30,680 --> 00:03:34,220
he tells us what he's going to do during the lesson.

51
00:03:34,220 --> 00:03:35,800
Woman: Right.

52
00:03:35,800 --> 00:03:41,900
Coleman: First, we usually spend about ten minutes doing some strumming.

53
00:03:41,900 --> 00:03:48,970
Woman: So is that using... what are they called... plectrums?

54
00:03:48,970 --> 00:03:52,720
Coleman: No - we just use our thumbs.

55
00:03:52,720 --> 00:03:54,850
Woman: Much easier.

56
00:03:54,850 --> 00:03:58,660
Coleman: Gary reminds us where to put our fingers for each chord

57
00:03:58,660 --> 00:04:01,600
and then we play them together.

58
00:04:01,600 --> 00:04:08,636
Coleman: Sometimes we all just start laughing because we're so bad at keeping time,

59
00:04:08,636 --> 00:04:11,920
so Gary starts clapping to help us.

60
00:04:11,920 --> 00:04:14,700
Woman: Do you learn to play any songs?

61
00:04:14,700 --> 00:04:19,780
Coleman: Yes - we do at least one song with words and chords

62
00:04:20,500 --> 00:04:23,250
I mean that's harder than you think.

63
00:04:23,250 --> 00:04:25,620
Woman: Oh, I'm sure it is!

64
00:04:25,620 --> 00:04:30,099
Coleman: That part of the lesson takes about 15 minutes.

65
00:04:30,099 --> 00:04:36,370
He often brings a recording of the song and plays it to us first.

66
00:04:36,370 --> 00:04:42,889
Coleman: Then he hands out the song and if there's a new chord in it,

67
00:04:42,889 --> 00:04:47,370
we practise that before we play it together - but really slowly.

68
00:04:47,370 --> 00:04:50,270
Woman: Do you do any fingerpicking?

69
00:04:50,270 --> 00:04:53,873
Coleman: That's the last ten minutes of the lesson,

70
00:04:53,873 --> 00:05:00,720
when we pick out the individual notes from a tune he's made up. It's always quite simple.

71
00:05:00,720 --> 00:05:02,750
Woman: That must be hard, though.

72
00:05:02,750 --> 00:05:06,011
Coleman: It is, but people like it

73
00:05:06,011 --> 00:05:08,260
because they can really concentrate

74
00:05:08,520 --> 00:05:13,465
and if we're all playing well, it sounds quite impressive.

75
00:05:13,465 --> 00:05:21,850
The only trouble is that he sometimes gets us to play one at a time - you know, alone.

76
00:05:21,850 --> 00:05:23,920
Woman: That's scary.

77
00:05:23,920 --> 00:05:28,371
Coleman: It is, but I've got used to it now.

78
00:05:28,371 --> 00:05:35,250
At the end he spends about five minutes telling us what to practise for the following week.

79
00:05:35,250 --> 00:05:49,420
Woman: Well, thanks Coleman. I'll go and have a look at that website, I think.
